# --- Agent working files ---------------------------------------------------
# Scratch, plan and progress-tracking documents an agent session may create while
# working in this tree. None is a project deliverable, and none belongs in the history.
#
# The patterns name exact files rather than globbing by concept, and that is the whole
# point of them being this explicit. docs/bring-up-log.md IS a log — it is also a
# required deliverable (FR-3.16) and the primary record of the hardware work. A pattern
# like *log*.md, *progress*, or docs/*log* would silently swallow it, and an ignored
# tracked file is invisible until someone notices it is missing. The documents this
# project ships are named descriptively (design-spec.md, measurements.md,
# bring-up-log.md, README.md), so nothing here can collide with one by accident.
#
# Deliberately NOT ignored: AGENTS.md. That is instructions for working in this
# repository rather than progress tracking, and it is meant to be committed and read.
PROGRESS.md
TODO.md
STATUS.md
HANDOFF.md
SESSION.md
SCRATCH.md
NOTES.md
PLAN.md
*.progress.md
*-progress.md
*-handoff.md
*-scratch.md
*-agent.md
agent.log
agent-logs/

# Agent scratch directories. harness/ is the path this project's own commit-history
# reconstruction used; the rest are the conventional spellings.
harness/
.harness/
.scratch/
scratch/
.agent/
.agents/

# Build trees. Two of them, both out of source: `build/` is the host tree that runs the
# unit tests, `build-arm/` is the cross-compiled firmware image.
build/
build-arm/
# A suffixed variant is a deliberate second tree, used to check the project builds
# under both ARM toolchains that can be selected (see cmake/arm-none-eabi.cmake).
build-arm-*/

# CMake's compile_commands.json is generated per build tree, and clang-tidy reads the
# copy inside build/. Nothing generated belongs in the repository.
compile_commands.json

# gtime, the commit-date walker. Its configuration is local to whoever is working in the
# tree and is no part of the deliverable, so neither filename it accepts is tracked.
# These sit at the repository root by default but the pattern has no slash, so a config
# placed elsewhere with --file is covered too. Its state is written to
# .git/gtime/state.json, which git cannot track and so needs no pattern.
#
# Both names are listed rather than just the first because gtime falls back to
# .gtime.json where tomllib is unavailable, and a file that is merely untracked is one
# `git add .` away from being committed.
.gtime.toml
.gtime.json

# Editors and tooling
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
